{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/279261-the-shadowrun-problem","id":279261,"title":"The shadowrun problem","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Demon: The Descent"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-10-12T18:35:38-07:00","id":279261,"title":"The shadowrun problem","content":"<i>20 minutes of objectives, 3 days of planning, and 600 seconds of mayhem...</i><br /> <br /> I just ran a con game, (while post the after-action report, time and energy permitting) with a full table of new players, no one had played anything by White Wolf/ Onyx path since oWoD, but after 30 minutes of explaining the setting (lot more dense than I realized) there was some initial trepidation, but by the first hour the players were slinging embeds, employing tradecraft and crafting Demon Pacts like old pros.<br /> <br /> They came on first major set-piece of the adventure: A cloud server farm that needed to be physically accessed to get the virtual server and the associated files.<br /> <br /> The challenges were pretty simple:<br /> <br /> 1. The network was sufficiently hardened that digital intrusion, by itself, wasn't a possibility.<br /> 2. the site was physically secure with no access to unauthorized personnel and competent, professional security staff (In story, this was explained as the influence of DHS acting as a puppet of the G-M). The building was constructed of concrete, bulletproof glass and other difficult to destroy material.<br /> 3. There was a G-M cultist on site that could summon a particularly powerful angel. As part of the pact the cultist had with the G-M, he couldn't leave the building, and lived in an extra dimensional space when he wasn't actively guarding the place.<br /> <br /> At this point, forward momentum on the adventure stopped as the players went to work on planning, planning, and more planning.<br /> <br /> I can complain too much, as players were clearly loving it, but something that meant to take, eh, 20-40 minutes of game time in a 4 hour slot wound up taking half of the time slot and becoming the major focus of the evening. Since I've run into this issue with my regular group whenever it's time to run a espionage adventure, I want to ask, has any one else run into this while running DtD? Is there anyway to keep heist planning from monopolizing play time?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2233-agentwestmer","name":"Agentwestmer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2233_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-12T18:39:28-07:00","id":279281,"title":"","content":"Considering I don't play a lot of one shots and have found the heist planning becomes a great point for character interaction as well as setting up dominos? Not particularly, beyond breaking up the steps of the heist and presenting bit by bit to them-give them short term objectives that are easier to plan for and then have them go for those.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-13T06:05:37-07:00","id":279575,"title":"","content":"An alternative would be a kind of In Medias Res approach where the &quot;heist&quot; is already happening and planning is resolved as short flashbacks. This turns long strategizing into thinking on your feet. The players have to quickly come up with plans (that their characters had thought up before) and ideas about how they &quot;were totally prepared for this&quot;. You might want to handle it with some sort of point-system to make sure the players do not end up &quot;prepared for everything&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Politeness is the lubricant of social intercourse.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/272-saibot","name":"saibot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar272_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1063"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-13T06:43:39-07:00","id":279594,"title":"","content":"The demons players guide had a method to run heists very similar to what sabout suggested","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/991-pgolasze13","name":"pgolasze13","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-21T07:35:17-07:00","id":288524,"title":"","content":"Well it depends on what exactly your goals are. <br /> <br /> If you want to remove the need for detailed planning, the mental combat system from Danse Macabre has a system based on physical combat for mental combat which would could cover such things as finding the holes in a security system. So using this system you could have decided in advance what the possible holes are and just require the demon PCs to beat the security chief or computer designer or whatever, and the plan would practically make itself. <br /> <br /> If you don't want to replace out of character planning but just shorten it to more manageable systems, Mirrors has an anticipation system for allowing PCs to have clever plans in their back pocket at the cost of a skill roll and a willpower point, so say the PCs might be holding a fake FBI badge to bluff their way through or a smoke grenade in their back pocket in need for a quick escape, and a rule for setups that allow players to go all Leverage at the cost of permanent willpower points (For example, with Set up, the guy who created the computer system was the demon's cultist so he knows the backdoor passwords, or the newest recruit to the security team is in fact the demons long lost son, that sort of thing) so the players can replace meticulous planning with thinking quickly on their feet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-26T07:31:08-07:00","id":292960,"title":"","content":"While I have not run any DtD games, I run a regular Hunter game and the players are meticulous planners. Well most of them are. There's at least one impulsive loose cannon who tends to act while everyone talks but in general the planning stages tend to be rich in role play and also give me a chance to feel out what the players intend so I can decide what elements should go to plan and what the surprises are. With exquisite planning they were able to infiltrate city hall, the flip side is that when they just went into a situation and improvised they ended up blowing up a Walmart. Which because this is an on going chronicle, has been an opportunity to teach about consequences for actions, even actions where the characters were not personally implicated.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1881-the-cowardly-scion","name":"The Cowardly Scion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1881_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-27T13:14:06-07:00","id":294072,"title":"","content":"Man, I absolutely love sessions like that! The PCs always get invested in their plan, and just building a Secure Area and telling them most of what's there doesn't take a ton of planning on my part,which leaves me free to do a bit of sneaky GM plotting while they're plotting their heist. It's a memorable, easy-to-run session which gives me time as an ST to focus on making something more complex or involved next week.<br /> <br /> But it can be unsatisfying as a pickup session, sometimes. Busting into a high security area in search of a McGuffin can fall flat in a pickup if the Players aren't immersed enough to buy into the McGuffin, or the McGuffin can feel like a letdown if after three hours of heist plotting you wind up with an incomplete victory (data that you still need to decrypt, just one part of the three-part artifact of doom, whatever). My favorite solutions to a heist-planning session taking up more of the game than it should? Have some antagonists bust in on the planning session! Have it become clear that whatever they're heisting is going to be destroyed/moved/used to locate every demon in the are/used to nuke Chicago in thirty minutes! Moving up the timetable or reminding the OCs that they are being hunted and probably shouldn't be sitting together in a large group in one place for long periods of time is a good way to get them to switch from planning mode to hit-the-ground-running mode.<br /> <br /> But PCs often enjoy a good heist, so don't do the whole &quot;no time to plan, we need to love NOW&quot; trick unless you need to. It works really great in an ongoing campaign that's had one of two heists already, as a change of pace.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-27T13:27:44-07:00","id":294085,"title":"","content":"One thing you can try (which might not be appropriate in every situation) is Chandler's Law - have someone or something attack the group, introducing a new complication that they have to deal with immediately. If that doesn't induce a feeling of urgency in the players, probably nothing will.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-27T21:08:43-07:00","id":294509,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wise Old Guru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/279261-the-shadowrun-problem?p=294072#post294072\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Man, I absolutely love sessions like that! The PCs always get invested in their plan, and just building a Secure Area and telling them most of what's there doesn't take a ton of planning on my part,which leaves me free to do a bit of sneaky GM plotting while they're plotting their heist. It's a memorable, easy-to-run session which gives me time as an ST to focus on making something more complex or involved next week.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, yes. My problem is every session turns into that. It's like having pizza every night, even if it's the best pizza in the world, sooner or later you get sick of it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2233-agentwestmer","name":"Agentwestmer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2233_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-27T21:59:32-07:00","id":294524,"title":"","content":"You can vary it up by going the In Medias Res rout, by having angels or something attack the players so they are on the defensive. Maybe make a few missions about determining the objectives or finding the target, instead of pulling off the heist. Also, try not to punish players too much when they don't plan.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1157-soulgambit","name":"SoulGambit","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"912"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-28T12:17:52-07:00","id":295065,"title":"","content":"Two things I suppose:<br /> <br /> 1) The only real fix is to discuss it with the players and work out what each of you are going to find interesting. You can force story elements but risk annoying the players; if they see an espionage game and look forward to spending long amounts of time meticulously planning and you drop in elements to disrupt that they will not be happy about it. But your fun is important too. I would sit down and work with them on how to put something together you both want.<br /> <br /> 2) You could also try having integrated NPCs in the party in games where they do this. Often I find GMs who dislike that partially dislike it because it leaves them sitting not doing anything for an hour or so while players talk. Integrated NPCs as part of the party may not make the final decision but can provide feedback and give you something to do during these planning sessions. You will just need to be sure of exactly what they know. If you already do this of course it does not help.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Moderator </span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\">Forum Rules</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#FF0000\">This is my mod voice.</span> This is my goth voice.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/15731-geist-balance-of-shadows-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">[Geist: Balance of Shadows</a><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/17537-v20-the-conspiracy-of-hrad-%C4%8Dern%C3%A1-hora-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\"> ][ Vampire: The Conspiracy of Hrad \u010cern\u00e1 Hora</a><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/13838-scion-bohemian-front-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\"> ][ Scion: Bohemian Front</a>][<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/99033-changeling-malibu-dream-house-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">Changeling: Malibu Dream House</a>] [<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/268069-demon-night-train-detective-agency-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">Demon: Night Train Detective Agency</a>] <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/la94fvk\" target=\"_blank\">[WoD: The Golden Eagle</a>]","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/56-baroness-nerak","name":"Baroness Nerak","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar56_2.jpeg","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2524"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}